WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.740
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.540
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:24.620
Angela Kinsey.

9
00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:25.660
Jenna Fisher.

10
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.680
I think I have a meta moment.

11
00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:35.560
Because today in the world, the 100th episode of Office Ladies podcast came out.

12
00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:36.120
Yes.

13
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:41.980
And now here we are recording our podcast about the 100th episode of The Office.

14
00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:43.460
That's very meta.

15
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.560
And our age is almost equal 100.

16
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.500
Oh, well, why'd you have to throw that in there?

17
00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:49.020
I don't know.

18
00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.380
I was looking for more hundreds.

19
00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:53.740
All right.

20
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.500
Well, we should get into this episode.

21
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.200
We have so much to cover.

22
00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:57.900
We do.

23
00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.600
This is going to be a long one.

24
00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:00.740
That's what she said.

25
00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:12.740
It's Company Picnic, Season 5, Episode 28, written by Jen Salata and Paul Lieberstein, directed by the dearest man on the planet, Ken Kwapis.

26
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:13.860
Summary.

27
00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:17.440
It's the Dunder Mifflin Company Picnic.

28
00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.200
All of them are there.

29
00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:20.180
All the branches.

30
00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:24.680
Yes, in their own special t-shirts that have their branch name on them.

31
00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:25.780
Oh, I have it, lady.

32
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:26.540
We'll get to it.

33
00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:32.960
Well, the Scranton branch is going to compete against the other branches in a company-wide volleyball tournament.

34
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:43.220
Meanwhile, Michael and Holly reunite to perform a comedy skit where they accidentally reveal some confidential information to the crowd.

35
00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:49.920
David Wallace, why would you tell Michael Scott anything of that magnitude and expect him to keep it quiet?

36
00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:57.600
When David introduces their skit, one of my favorite lines is when he says, I have not seen this.

37
00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:58.440
I have not seen this.

38
00:01:58.560 --> 00:01:58.660
Yeah.

39
00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:05.220
Finally, Dwight is going to defend Angela's honor.

40
00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:06.620
Tell Rolf.

41
00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:07.440
Yeah.

42
00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:07.940
Yeah.

43
00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:15.340
And ultimately, an ankle injury is going to send Pam to the hospital where she and Jim get some surprising news.

44
00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:16.440
So sweet.

45
00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:17.720
I love this episode.

46
00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:24.200
Fast fact number one, Company Picnic was the 100th episode of The Office.

47
00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:25.220
Yes.

48
00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:31.360
This was a very big deal for the network because it meant our show was now eligible for syndication.

49
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.320
Which I didn't even know what that meant.

50
00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.160
I thought maybe we should explain it.

51
00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:35.580
Okay.

52
00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:35.980
Do.

53
00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:39.020
Because I remember when we hit 100, I was like, what's all the hullabaloo?

54
00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:39.520
Yeah, sure.

55
00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:40.060
We hit 100.

56
00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:40.580
That's cool.

57
00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:41.020
I know.

58
00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:43.900
Maybe, you know, we'll all go out to dinner or something.

59
00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:44.660
No.

60
00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:44.980
No.

61
00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:46.940
For the network, super big.

62
00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:48.540
They all showed up to the set.

63
00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:49.340
They did.

64
00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:51.140
They brought a cake.

65
00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:54.000
It was as big as like a poker table.

66
00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:55.460
It was humongous.

67
00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:55.980
It was.

68
00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:56.660
That's what she said.

69
00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:57.940
That's what she said.

70
00:02:58.040 --> 00:02:58.940
They brought champagne.

71
00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:00.500
But we couldn't drink it.

72
00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.160
No, because we had to go back to work.

73
00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:07.360
We actually only had 15 minutes with the cake, all said and told.

74
00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:08.760
It was on our lunch break.

75
00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:13.680
So if you wanted to eat, we had 30 minutes, photo with a cake, and then we had to like run out the door.

76
00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:16.700
Well, I remember Greg gave a very nice speech.

77
00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:17.160
He did.

78
00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.360
About what the 100 episodes had meant to him.

79
00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:20.740
Entertainment Tonight was there.

80
00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:21.940
All the press was there.

81
00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:23.320
I mean, this was a super big deal.

82
00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:23.720
Mm-hmm.

83
00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.200
Let's tell you why.

84
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:38.020
Anyway, when a show hits 100 episodes and goes into syndication, it means that the network can now start selling the show in a package to different places where they can rerun it.

85
00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:41.500
So it doesn't just run on NBC anymore.

86
00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:43.940
It gets on all the cable networks.

87
00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.740
Like Comedy Central, how it reruns on Comedy Central.

88
00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:47.260
Right.

89
00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:51.080
And guys, remember, this predates all streaming.

90
00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:51.900
Yes.

91
00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:54.160
So this was all kind of a cable thing.

92
00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:57.160
But this meant big money for the network.

93
00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:02.120
I didn't know that, but it makes sense because these other places pay them to have it.

94
00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:02.580
Yes.

95
00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:12.000
But what I think a lot of people don't know is that the actors do not cash in on this unless they are what is called profit participants.

96
00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:12.660
Oh, yeah.

97
00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:13.220
And guess what?

98
00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:13.720
We weren't.

99
00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:14.400
We were not.

100
00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:20.320
Now, our union, SAG, does negotiate a small fee for syndicated reruns.

101
00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:22.460
So it's very fun.

102
00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:24.900
They're great.

103
00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:27.160
But they have, you guys, this, like, cascading tier.

104
00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:28.640
So you get a check and you're like, whoop-hoo.

105
00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:29.960
Then the next one's a little lower.

106
00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:30.900
Then it's a little lower.

107
00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:31.960
Then it's a little lower.

108
00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:32.960
Then it's a little lower.

109
00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:33.960
Then it's a little lower.

110
00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:34.580
Yes.

111
00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:35.780
And then it's for, like, $2.

112
00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:40.480
Well, you know that bar that they have called Residuals in Burbank?

113
00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.220
This is a famous bar, you guys.

114
00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:42.580
Yeah.

115
00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:44.520
So there's this bar called Residuals.

116
00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:50.360
And if you're an actor and you bring in a residual check that's under $1, they'll give you a free drink.

117
00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:52.800
And then they tack your check up on the wall.

118
00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:53.260
Yeah.

119
00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:57.800
I have a friend of mine who saved a residual check for, I think it was $0.17.

120
00:04:58.920 --> 00:04:59.180
Yes.

121
00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:00.620
And, like, put it on his bulletin board.

122
00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.540
Lady, I got one for $0.21.

123
00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.660
It costs more money to mail it.

124
00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:05.240
I know.

125
00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:05.620
To me.

126
00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:06.620
Probably.

127
00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:07.580
And to print it.

128
00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:08.180
Yeah.

129
00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:08.540
Yeah.

130
00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:09.540
Exactly.

131
00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:13.260
So, yeah, they were able to make a lot of money selling the show to cable.

132
00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:17.940
And then eventually, you guys, I mean, you know, they were able to sell it to all the streamers.

133
00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:18.460
Yeah.

134
00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:23.340
I guess we should probably say the actors don't get a big payout for that either.

135
00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:26.140
That money goes to the profit participants.

136
00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:29.940
See, the word profit participant is something I had never heard of.

137
00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:35.260
And as it turns out, if you're one of those folks, that is the shiitake.

138
00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:36.300
Mm-hmm.

139
00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:37.160
Mm-hmm.

140
00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:37.960
Mm-hmm.

141
00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:38.840
It sure is.

142
00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:46.400
Angela, you found out the inspiration for our 100th episode while listening to the DVD commentary.

143
00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:47.740
Will you share?

144
00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:48.580
I will.

145
00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:50.760
I'm officially part of Fast Fact No.

146
00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:51.540
1, folks.

147
00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:52.020
Oh, yeah.

148
00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:52.960
Here's the thing.

149
00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:58.480
If you love really good DVD commentaries, this is one not to miss of the Office box set.

150
00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:03.340
Because Ken Quapis and Paul Lieberstein and Jen Salata share so many great details.

151
00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:08.500
They are the only three people on this commentary, and I really enjoyed it.

152
00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:13.920
Paul shared that the idea for the company picnic, they had it at the very beginning of season five.

153
00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:17.260
They always knew that they would be working towards this episode.

154
00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:18.040
Wow.

155
00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:22.240
And Jen shared that they wanted to bring all of the branches together.

156
00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:24.760
They researched company picnics.

157
00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:29.500
This delighted me, because I just imagined Jen Googling company picnics.

158
00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:31.260
She was a lady who Googled.

159
00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:33.560
I was going to say, that would have been us.

160
00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:36.560
We would have been like, wait, let's research what people do.

161
00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:39.740
And one of the things that came up was a volleyball tournament.

162
00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:41.440
So they thought that would be fun.

163
00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:45.940
And Paul said he thought it was a great way to bring everyone back together, all the different

164
00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:49.920
branches, all the different characters for this big 100th episode.

165
00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:55.340
The only characters you don't see that were in the world of the show really were Jan.

166
00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:57.440
But it doesn't make sense for Jan to come back.

167
00:06:57.640 --> 00:06:57.880
Yes.

168
00:06:58.060 --> 00:06:58.660
And Karen.

169
00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:00.460
Yes, we got mail about that.

170
00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:01.360
Where was Karen?

171
00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:08.780
And Jen Salata did an interview where she said Rashida Jones was filming Parks and Recreation,

172
00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:12.580
and they couldn't get her for a whole week of work to be at this picnic.

173
00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:16.920
And then they realized, wait, she would be on maternity leave.

174
00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:21.980
So they tracked her story-wise as being on maternity leave, and that's why she wasn't there.

175
00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:23.420
Well, that worked out perfect.

176
00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:28.760
While the volleyball was a great idea, Ken did share that it was a logistical nightmare

177
00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:33.420
because they started filming and they were trying to keep with the rules of volleyball,

178
00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:39.200
which means the teams trade sides depending on score or whatever, but they just had to

179
00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:39.520
bail.

180
00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:42.580
They had to bail because it just got too complicated.

181
00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:48.060
And Jen said she had this memory of looking over at Ken, and he had a clipboard, and he

182
00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:54.720
had a little quarter, and he was tracing this coin with a pencil and placing it and mapping

183
00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:59.500
out where everyone was going to stand and who was serving and who was playing what position

184
00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:02.400
because it was so complex to shoot.

185
00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:06.620
I thought it was interesting that they chose volleyball.

186
00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:13.000
Any company picnic I've ever been to, it's usually softball, but I do see how that people are

187
00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:14.360
really spread out in softball.

188
00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:16.120
So I guess that makes sense to me.

189
00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:16.580
Right.

190
00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:18.600
But I mean, I can't even imagine.

191
00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:20.400
Jen and Paul shared this other thing.

192
00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:25.720
Jen said that her and Paul, as they wrote this episode, they thought that each team had

193
00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:26.500
nine players.

194
00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:30.660
And so they wrote the whole script with each team having nine players.

195
00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:31.280
Oh, no.

196
00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:35.260
And then they got to the production meeting, and in the production meeting, someone was

197
00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:40.100
like, you know, on volleyball teams, you have six players on the court, not nine.

198
00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:41.560
And Paul and them were like, what?

199
00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:44.760
Gosh.

200
00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:47.140
But yeah, so they had to change that immediately.

201
00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:50.560
And I have more fun tidbits from the three of them in the commentary.

202
00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:51.880
I'll sprinkle through the episode.

203
00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:52.660
All right.

204
00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:53.380
Angela.

205
00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:53.860
What?

206
00:08:54.020 --> 00:08:55.180
Great job on that fast fact.

207
00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:56.080
Oh, thank you.

208
00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:56.920
I really enjoyed that.

209
00:08:57.340 --> 00:08:57.940
Thanks so much.

210
00:08:57.940 --> 00:08:58.560
You told me.

211
00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:02.160
You were like, Jenna, there's some great stuff about the inspiration behind the episode.

212
00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:05.440
And I said, I'm holding a spot for you up in Fast Facts, lady.

213
00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:05.960
You did.

214
00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:07.020
All right.

215
00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:11.340
Fast fact number two is a location breakdown.

216
00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:18.000
Now, the signs hanging in this episode say that this picnic is being held at the Meadows

217
00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:19.480
in upstate New York.

218
00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:24.300
I could not find any information on this or if this is a real place.

219
00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:24.760
Hmm.

220
00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:26.020
Fakie place.

221
00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:26.860
Perhaps.

222
00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:28.080
Does anyone know?

223
00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:31.160
Please comment on Office Ladies Pod.

224
00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:35.100
If you've ever been to a park called the Meadows in upstate New York, I'd love to know.

225
00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:36.380
Fan question.

226
00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:42.220
Rayyan from Pakistan would like to know, where did we actually film this episode?

227
00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:46.180
Well, we filmed at Calamigos Ranch in Malibu Canyon.

228
00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:49.160
It is a beautiful location.

229
00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:53.940
It's kind of nestled into the side of this canyon in the Malibu Mountains.

230
00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:59.760
Did you know it has its own water source from a spring on the property?

231
00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:03.220
I didn't know that, but I know a lot of people get married there.

232
00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:04.820
I've been to a wedding there.

233
00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:05.820
You know what?

234
00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:11.000
Randy Cordray knew about this location because he had been the best man at a wedding there.

235
00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:15.940
And when the script came up, he remembered it and suggested it to our locations manager,

236
00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:16.840
Kyle Alexander.

237
00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:20.120
And they scouted it and they all agreed it was perfect.

238
00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:25.540
So now, Angela, you mentioned how Ken Kwapis said like this was a logistically difficult

239
00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:26.160
shoot.

240
00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:26.660
Mm-hmm.

241
00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:32.420
Randy said this was a very difficult episode to pull off logistically from a production

242
00:10:32.420 --> 00:10:35.320
side because we had so many people.

243
00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:38.140
So many people and we were in the middle of a canyon.

244
00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:42.720
He said we had to hire additional crew, including multiple medics.

245
00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:47.340
And we had hydration stations and sunscreen stations.

246
00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:50.280
And Sergio and Alan are caterers.

247
00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:52.080
They had to triple their staff.

248
00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:56.600
They had three lines to serve lunch, like three buffet lines.

249
00:10:57.140 --> 00:11:03.660
And then Peter and Vartan at craft service, they also had three stations instead of one.

250
00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:09.680
It was a lot of concern about being able to feed everybody because all the background food,

251
00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:11.500
people were told not to eat.

252
00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:12.840
Yeah, it was fakie food.

253
00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:18.700
It was real food, but it had been at various temperatures that made it sort of unsafe to

254
00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:18.860
eat.

255
00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:24.340
